  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x-2} & ={7}^{3-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({5}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({7}^{3-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)=(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5) & =(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)) & =2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
